WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.580
- Let's take a step back, so,
the reason that we do that

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:05.250
is so we can have more
authentic relationships.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:08.240
And not be looking at
our phone like you said,

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.430
when you're at a restaurant,
when they open them back up,

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:12.707
I love that idea, everybody
stacks their phone

00:00:12.707 --> 00:00:15.920
and the first person to
look at it pays the bill.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.007
That's fantastic.

00:00:17.007 --> 00:00:18.550
- [Dave] You're paying the bill, yeah.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:20.770
- We need to get back
to these face-to-face,

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:23.530
to actually listening to
people and hearing them

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:24.849
and being heard.

00:00:24.849 --> 00:00:28.099
(rhythmic synth music)

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:35.890
Hi, my name's Peter Boghossian from

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:39.620
the Don't Burn This Book
Club, with Dave Rubin,

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.997
and this is chapter 10,
Move On with Your Life,

00:00:41.997 --> 00:00:45.160
and it is a genuine pleasure to do this.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.860
I love the book, I love chapter 10,

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:50.030
and I'm just gonna jump right in, Dave,

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:52.400
and I'm gonna ask you some questions.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.930
So, first, in your
opinion, one of the things

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:58.330
that you said in the book
that's been tormenting me

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:01.210
since I wrote it was how
you put away your devices

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:02.493
and you get off the grid.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:04.413
Tell me a bit--

00:01:04.413 --> 00:01:05.246
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:01:06.438 --> 00:01:07.680
- How do you that?

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.770
I mean, like, even my phone,
I even use as an alarm

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.610
to wake me up, I don't
have an alarm clock,

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:13.970
I look at the time.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:15.730
How do you do that?

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:20.270
You also mention don't put
your phone in your room

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:22.790
because the bedroom isn't for that.

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:24.533
How do you get started with that?

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.140
- Yeah, so, first off, I just wanna say

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:28.880
thank you for doing
this, Pete, and you were,

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.160
when we looked at the 10 people

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.510
that I wanted to hit these 10 chapters,

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:35.280
I knew that you were the guy
I wanted to do chapter 10 with

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.410
because our friendship
and I think the things

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:41.070
that you've tried to
put out into the world

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:44.134
for whatever our minor political
disagreements might be,

00:01:44.134 --> 00:01:46.950
you wanna live in the same
place that I wanna live in

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.630
and you've stood up to
the mob in so many ways.

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:53.730
For those that don't know,
you're up in Portland,

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:57.260
that's not an easy place for
an anti-social justice warrior

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.330
free-thinker to be, so you've
been in the thick of it.

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:03.060
So, to answer your question
specifically, well, look,

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:05.240
first off, I lay out a
couple tips and tricks

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.070
you can do to get rid of
the phone because right now,

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:10.950
although I wrote this,
obviously, before coronavirus,

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:13.420
it's like we were all
obsessed with the digital life

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:16.830
and big tech was already
in control of so much

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:20.600
of our information before corona
and now we're in lockdown,

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.270
and it looks like in certain states,

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:25.050
probably our two states,
we're gonna be in this thing

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:26.650
for quite some time.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:31.650
Big tech and Twitter and YouTube
and videos and information,

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:35.760
it has more of a hold on us
right now than it had two months

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.560
ago when it already had
too much control of us.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.480
So I do lay out a couple of tips.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:42.750
I mean, one thing, you might have to get

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:43.770
an alarm clock, Pete.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.740
I know it feels very old-school but yeah,

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.750
we all use our phone now as the alarm.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:53.183
So that means you probably
bring it into your bedroom.

00:02:53.183 --> 00:02:54.016
- I do.

00:02:54.016 --> 00:02:55.670
- But one of the things
that I've found, we all do,

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:57.510
but one of the things that I've found is,

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.010
if you bring that thing into your bedroom,

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.400
it will be the last thing
you look at at night

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.200
and it will be the first
thing that you look at

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:05.033
in the morning.

00:03:05.033 --> 00:03:07.430
And for guys like us especially,

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:10.030
it is not good to start
your day and open up Twitter

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:14.143
and have an anime pink
fox tell you that you're,

00:03:14.143 --> 00:03:16.760
yeah, that you're Hitler, in essence.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:20.650
That's not what you should be
seeing before you go to bed.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:22.360
So I do believe that the bedroom,

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.300
we don't have a television
in our bedroom, either.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:25.550
I believe that the bedroom

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:27.530
is basically for sex and sleeping.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:30.140
You can probably add a couple
other little things in there.

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:32.960
You can read, read a
book in bed, that's good,

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:34.200
or on your Kindle.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.660
But I think figuring
out the little moments

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:42.660
to detach from all of this
is just super important.

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:44.840
- Right, and so, let's take a step back.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:47.260
So the reason that we do
that is so we can have

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:50.010
more authentic relationships
and not be looking

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:53.040
at our phone like you said,
when you're at a restaurant,

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:55.118
when they open them back up,
I love that idea, you know,

00:03:55.118 --> 00:03:57.500
everybody stacks their
phone and the first person

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.580
to look at it pays the
bill, that's fantastic.

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:02.130
- [Dave] You're paying the bill, yeah.

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:04.580
- Yeah, we need to get
back to these face-to-face,

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:07.360
to actually listening to
people and hearing them

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:08.820
and being heard.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:13.820
My problem, though, is, so I'm
trying to walk 12,500 steps

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:16.580
a day and I was thinking, well,
jeez, I'll leave my phone.

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:19.220
But if I left my phone, I
wouldn't listen to your podcasts

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:22.333
or podcasts or music or a book.
Do you know what I'm saying?

00:04:22.333 --> 00:04:25.480
It's like they're all,
the phone is so integrated

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:29.030
into our lives but yet, and you're right,

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:30.630
you mention in the book
that it's an addiction.

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:32.850
I think we all have a
low-grade, or some people

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:34.250
have a high-grade addiction.

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:37.950
What advice can you give people

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:42.280
to really bracket
technology in their lives?

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:46.490
Like, you've gone off the grid for weeks.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:48.680
We know why you should do it,

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:51.040
to re-inject the humanism
in our relationships.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:53.060
How do you get started with that?

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:54.860
'Cause I'm trying to figure that out.

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:56.440
I don't know how to do it.

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:58.070
- I think the first thing is realize

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:02.430
that we don't have to be
endlessly entertained or amused.

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:04.850
So, you're a dog guy,
you've got a couple dogs.

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:05.800
One of the things that I do,

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:07.430
Clyde's sitting right here right now,

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:08.730
when I take him out for a walk,

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:10.130
I do not take my phone.

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:13.380
So, yes, that means I am
not listening to a podcast.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:15.290
I have a lot of friends
who are podcasters,

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:16.570
there's podcasts that I enjoy,

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:18.230
but I'm not listening to a podcast.

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:19.800
I'm not looking at my phone.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.840
I'm not waiting for emails to roll in.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:23.520
And we take a walk.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:25.910
And you know, it's so
funny because that sounds

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:26.743
like a unique thing,

00:05:26.743 --> 00:05:28.930
my God, you walk your dog with no device.

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:31.110
It sounds like some sort of crazy thing.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:34.350
That is how we all lived
a mere 20 years ago.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:35.183
Let's not forget.

00:05:35.183 --> 00:05:37.940
I got my first cell phone,
it was a little Nokia,

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:38.880
one of those little bars,

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:40.060
that all you could do was play snake.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:42.570
I got that thing in 2000, that's
when I got my first phone.

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:44.080
Only 20 years ago.

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:47.160
So, part of the issue related to tech,

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:49.670
and why we all sort of
feel crazy all the time,

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:52.640
and sometimes you're on
your phone and your wife

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:54.460
or your husband, whatever it is,

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:56.710
is literally talking to you
and you hear them talking

00:05:56.710 --> 00:05:58.970
but you don't respond, and
we're all guilty of this.

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:00.920
You go, "What, can you repeat that?"

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:02.610
And it's like, man, that's what people do

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:05.173
when they're 80 and 90.

00:06:06.047 --> 00:06:09.080
And I think it's done all
sorts of stuff to re-wire us,

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:13.070
to short-out our attention
span, and all of these things.

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:15.640
So I think partly, I think the first step

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:19.560
is just realizing that just
because we have this stuff

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:21.810
doesn't mean we should
need it all the time.

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:24.400
Now, I say that as someone,
I admit this in chapter 10,

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:25.900
I have a terrible sense of direction.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:28.300
So when I went off the grid in August,

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:31.410
well, I still do have GPS
in my car so it's not that

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:33.540
I had no screen whatsoever.

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:35.153
But even on the news front,

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:39.320
I've taken these last three
Augusts where I do no news.

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:40.840
And guess what?

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:42.170
You don't miss much.

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:44.980
Now, August is a little down, anyway.

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:46.800
The year before last, I believe,

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.610
it was when John McCain died,
I didn't hear about that.

00:06:49.610 --> 00:06:51.610
Then there was three
weeks of hearing about

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:53.930
Michael Cohen testimony against Trump.

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:55.695
It's like you realize that these things,

00:06:55.695 --> 00:06:59.016
we feel like they're so
important on any given moment,

00:06:59.016 --> 00:07:03.360
and then you realize it
doesn't really matter.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:05.300
And that's why I start
the chapter with the story

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:07.893
about the Australian woman.
- I was just gonna say that.

00:07:07.893 --> 00:07:10.590
Yeah, you started the
chapter with something

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:13.970
that we've all heard about hospice stay

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:16.695
and what people value toward
the end of their lives

00:07:16.695 --> 00:07:18.964
and what gives their lives meaning.

00:07:18.964 --> 00:07:21.610
I wanna know, so when
you do go off the grid,

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:24.060
and you don't talk to people
when you have your device,

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:26.310
do you feel that you gain more meaning--

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:27.143
- [Dave] Yes.

00:07:27.143 --> 00:07:29.020
- And you feel more
closely connected, yeah.

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:32.900
- Yes, I feel more patient,
I feel more calm, I know,

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:35.950
you know, I'm pretty
friendly, I'm fairly outgoing

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:38.930
in general but I know
that when I'm at a store,

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:41.952
I'm friendlier with the cashier.

00:07:41.952 --> 00:07:43.560
Normally you can go to a cashier,

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:45.723
you're on Twitter the whole
time, you don't even look,

00:07:45.723 --> 00:07:48.220
in some ways you don't
even realize it's a human.

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:50.117
But when you go there without a phone,

00:07:50.117 --> 00:07:51.947
"Oh, hi, how are ya,
nice to see you again,

00:07:51.947 --> 00:07:53.210
"how's your day going?"

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:55.173
And then people start looking at you.

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:58.130
- Right, and so let me ask you a question.

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:01.290
So, then, for you, in your
life, the thing that gives

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:06.290
the most meaning, is that
your interactions with people?

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:07.740
Like your relationships with people?

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:10.683
Like relationships with your
husband and your friends?

00:08:10.683 --> 00:08:15.683
Is that ultimately, to
you, what it's about?

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:19.560
- You know, I think ultimately
that is what it's about.

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:21.440
I've had a lot of time,
just like everybody else,

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:24.370
to think about my life while
we're in quarantine here.

00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:26.100
And one of the things that
I've been thinking about

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:30.640
is I'm very satisfied and I
would say blessed and lucky

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:33.210
that I live in the house
that I wanna live in.

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:35.450
I don't really want any physical things.

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:37.510
We don't have a ton of stuff
but we have the things,

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:39.470
we share one car, it's a nice car,

00:08:39.470 --> 00:08:41.183
but I don't want more cars.

00:08:42.852 --> 00:08:45.230
There really aren't
physical things that I want.

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:47.880
Now, that's not to say I don't
wanna be more successful,

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:50.410
I don't wanna continue to enrich my mind

00:08:50.410 --> 00:08:51.860
and continue to have an influence

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:54.300
in all of the things that we do.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:59.093
But my relationship with
my partner is pretty great.

00:08:59.093 --> 00:09:01.590
I've been working harder,
even through writing this,

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:02.850
I think it's made me a better person,

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:05.280
so I've been working harder
at some of the interpersonal

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:07.430
family stuff and that kind of thing.

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:09.780
And I think a lot of people
are feeling this right now.

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:12.300
In the midst of quarantine,
it hit me the other day,

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:15.680
it'll be really nice when
I can see my parents again

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:17.810
and hug them, just literally hug them.

00:09:17.810 --> 00:09:20.300
We Facetime all the time
but just to hug them.

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:24.150
And I know you lost your
dad in the last few years

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:28.380
and it's like we all forget
that this is just temporary.

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:31.440
And we're so caught up in
nonsense that that's why

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:34.750
I started the chapter
like that, talking about

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:36.710
this woman taking care
of these hospice patients

00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:38.970
because nobody said, "I
wanted to be at work more."

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:41.460
Nobody said, "I wish I had paid
more attention to politics."

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:43.747
Nobody said, "Oh, I
should've been fighting

00:09:43.747 --> 00:09:45.240
"with my neighbor more."

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:46.477
Every single person said,

00:09:46.477 --> 00:09:48.327
"I should've followed my passion more.

00:09:48.327 --> 00:09:49.747
"I should've done what I wanted to do.

00:09:49.747 --> 00:09:52.340
"I should've followed that
love that I let get away."

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:53.173
And all those things.

00:09:53.173 --> 00:09:54.450
And it's like, we know this.

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:56.840
This is not rocket science, we know it.

00:09:56.840 --> 00:09:57.870
- Right.

00:09:57.870 --> 00:09:59.550
So let me ask you a question.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:04.160
The theme that emerges
is let friends be wrong.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:08.390
And you have friends,
people you have significant

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:11.310
political disagreements with
and they're still your friends.

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:14.448
And for some people, that
idea is just like a toxin.

00:10:14.448 --> 00:10:17.010
Like they're just incredulous.

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:19.950
My guess is it would be
with folks who are younger.

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:23.070
How do you navigate those
differences with your friends

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:25.500
if they have different,
'cause you're a political guy

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:26.930
and you think about this stuff.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:29.710
How do you navigate those
differences with your friends?

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:34.230
People who have
substantive, maybe, I guess,

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:36.223
how do you do that and are
there beliefs that people have

00:10:36.223 --> 00:10:39.540
that would be a deal breaker
for you in your relationships?

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:41.300
- Yeah, there's a guy that
I know that wrote a book,

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:42.180
I have it right over here.

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:44.360
It's called "How to Have
Impossible Conversations."

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:46.390
It's a pretty good read.
(laughing)

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:49.530
And you're somebody that's
been trying to do this

00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:51.490
and you go to public places sometimes

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:53.380
and you welcome people to argue with you

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:55.350
and counterpoint with you and all that.

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:58.160
I actually don't think
that our position on this,

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:01.840
to agree to disagree, is really against

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:02.980
what most people believe.

00:11:02.980 --> 00:11:04.980
I think the people that
don't believe it right now,

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:07.770
or let's say the people who
are so angry all the time

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:08.940
and try to de-platform us.

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:11.480
I mean, we've done events
together where we had

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:13.800
protestors calling us
Nazis and try to shut down

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:16.860
the event and call fire
alarms and the whole thing.

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:18.260
I don't think they really believe it.

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:20.490
I think they're scared and I also think

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:24.380
that their set of ideas is so conflicting

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:27.130
and so compounding and I always say this,

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:28.750
in many ways it's anti-human.

00:11:28.750 --> 00:11:30.510
You know, this idea of social justice

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:33.220
and this idea of grouping
people, it's anti-human.

00:11:33.220 --> 00:11:36.240
Because what's human is to
be an individual, right?

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:38.270
You're Peter Boghossian,
who should be judged

00:11:38.270 --> 00:11:40.470
on your ideas and actions and thoughts.

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:45.030
You're not just, oh, middle-aged
white guy, must think this.

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:48.303
And I think what happens,
why so many of them go crazy,

00:11:48.303 --> 00:11:52.410
is because once you take a set of ideas

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:55.210
and apply it to yourself
that is actually against you,

00:11:55.210 --> 00:11:58.330
it's against you as a
person, as an individual,

00:11:58.330 --> 00:12:00.120
you start getting more hysterical.

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:04.340
So what I try to do, and
it is why I started doing

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:06.260
the off-the-grid thing
and why I try not to tweet

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:08.460
on the weekends, which I
haven't don't that well

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:11.520
during coronavirus 'cause
the weekend is sort of,

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:13.360
the week and the weekend have blended

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:15.644
into one endless thing.

00:12:15.644 --> 00:12:20.644
But the reason that I try to
do it is we shouldn't always

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:23.740
be doing this thing.

00:12:23.740 --> 00:12:25.320
You shouldn't feel, oh,

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:27.490
it's why I only do one or
two shows a week, by the way.

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:29.187
People always say to me,
"Dave, why don't you do

00:12:29.187 --> 00:12:30.300
"five interviews a week?"

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:32.640
Now, I know we could do more
and I'd make more money.

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:35.070
And we could do all that kind of stuff.

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:36.390
And it's not for a lack of working,

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:38.180
I bust my ass every second of the day.

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:41.900
It's because I don't
wanna keep hitting people

00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:42.733
with this stuff.

00:12:42.733 --> 00:12:45.620
It's like, if you give
me an hour of your week,

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:49.050
if I have one solid interview
with whoever it might be,

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:50.620
and you give me an hour of your week,

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:53.330
well, then, I assume you
might have given Sam Harris

00:12:53.330 --> 00:12:55.540
some time in your week, you
might have given Joe Rogan

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:56.520
some time in your week.

00:12:56.520 --> 00:12:59.207
The litany of other people
and not just politics,

00:12:59.207 --> 00:13:01.350
whatever you do.

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:04.410
And I want people to do
all those other things.

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:08.140
Care about the world, but
care about yourself first.

00:13:08.140 --> 00:13:09.937
I think that really is the message.

00:13:09.937 --> 00:13:12.450
- And that makes you less
attentive to the people

00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:15.520
you have if you're cramming
in more people, right?

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:19.823
So, it makes those interactions,
I would think, less,

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:23.900
you'd be less present
for them, is that right?

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:26.250
- Well, absolutely, and
the other thing here,

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:29.940
and I think this is probably
a very Jordan Peterson notion,

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:32.730
and I know that, since you read the book,

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:34.040
you know chapter nine and the way

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:35.790
that Jordan has affected me.

00:13:35.790 --> 00:13:37.990
But a lot of times I'll
see, because we all get

00:13:37.990 --> 00:13:40.850
so much hate online,
and it's just devolved

00:13:40.850 --> 00:13:42.700
into something that it
wasn't supposed to be.

00:13:42.700 --> 00:13:45.370
It was supposed to be social
media, make us social,

00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:46.880
and we're social creatures
and we all thought

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:48.330
it was gonna build something amazing

00:13:48.330 --> 00:13:49.920
and not to say it hasn't.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:52.090
Look at our lives, how
they've been enriched,

00:13:52.090 --> 00:13:53.780
we know each other because of this thing.

00:13:53.780 --> 00:13:57.150
My show, I'm not denying
that it's been good.

00:13:57.150 --> 00:13:59.540
But I would say social
media and the internet,

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:00.420
you know, it's like fire.

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:02.187
Fire can cook and it can warm you up

00:14:02.187 --> 00:14:05.340
and it can also burn the house
down and kill the family.

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:09.700
So it's sort of like that but
one of the things that I see,

00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:13.350
which is why I think getting
out of this thing is important,

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:16.330
is that all the people that
are sort of the endless haters,

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:18.360
all the anonymous accounts and the people

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:19.301
that just wanna destroy all the time

00:14:19.301 --> 00:14:20.460
- [Pete] Sock puppets.

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:22.090
- and silence everybody.

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:26.220
It's like, if you guys
would just clean your room,

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:27.590
that's what Jordan's message was.

00:14:27.590 --> 00:14:30.440
Meaning, I would guarantee
you that most of the people

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:34.510
that spend all day long sowing
chaos across the internet,

00:14:34.510 --> 00:14:37.970
it's because whatever
their internal stuff is.

00:14:37.970 --> 00:14:39.000
We've all had it, right?

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:40.790
We've all had it, we all have it.

00:14:40.790 --> 00:14:42.860
But if you don't solve
your internal stuff,

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:45.040
you will send the world into chaos.

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:47.550
And that's why I think his
message really resonated

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:48.730
with so many people.

00:14:48.730 --> 00:14:51.340
Because it was like, set
some order in your life

00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:54.220
and then you might set
some order in the world.

00:14:54.220 --> 00:14:57.360
And I think partly not being obsessed

00:14:57.360 --> 00:14:59.550
with the online adventure
and understanding

00:14:59.550 --> 00:15:01.840
that we're organic
creatures in a real world,

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:04.410
I think that's part of how you do it.

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:07.500
- Let me ask you two questions,
two finishing questions,

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:10.390
about the process of writing the book.

00:15:10.390 --> 00:15:14.370
What did you learn from the
process of writing this book?

00:15:14.370 --> 00:15:16.347
'Cause I know people are
out there listening, like,

00:15:16.347 --> 00:15:18.750
"Jeez, I'd like to write a book."

00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:21.070
What have you learned from this?

00:15:21.070 --> 00:15:23.420
- I love that you're asking me that.

00:15:23.420 --> 00:15:25.340
Well, first off, if you're
thinking about writing a book,

00:15:25.340 --> 00:15:27.630
just start doing it.

00:15:27.630 --> 00:15:29.200
I know it sounds simple.

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:30.870
It's like, what kind of advice is that?

00:15:30.870 --> 00:15:32.740
It's like, "I wanna write a
book." "Yeah, start doing it."

00:15:32.740 --> 00:15:33.573
"Okay, no shit."

00:15:33.573 --> 00:15:36.650
But it's like, really, just like, sit down

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:40.160
and sit at that computer
and pump out a couple things

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:42.590
that you think are important
and then see if you can just

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:44.970
make them into even
essays, whatever they are.

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:46.500
Everyone has a different process.

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:47.880
You were one of the people that I called

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:50.030
every now and again to kinda work through

00:15:50.030 --> 00:15:51.920
an issue as they were coming.

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:54.310
What I realized for me is my process,

00:15:54.310 --> 00:15:58.030
I would get up in the morning,
Emma, our previous dog,

00:15:58.030 --> 00:16:00.300
was still alive, but she
was in her last days,

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:03.210
I would get her out to pee,
she'd come into the room

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:07.300
with me and she'd lay there
and I'd have my coffee

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:08.420
and then I would go.

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:10.430
And it would be at about 7:00 a.m. usually

00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:13.910
and I could go sometimes 'til
2:00 p.m. without stopping.

00:16:13.910 --> 00:16:15.210
Sometimes forgetting to eat.

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:16.043
I've been telling people,

00:16:16.043 --> 00:16:17.990
David would literally open up the door,

00:16:17.990 --> 00:16:20.970
slide a sandwich in on
a plate as if I was in

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:24.030
maximum security prison,

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:25.660
and sometimes I wouldn't even eat that.

00:16:25.660 --> 00:16:27.710
I could go and go and go and
then when I hit the wall,

00:16:27.710 --> 00:16:31.103
I was done, I would not
start again at five o'clock.

00:16:31.103 --> 00:16:32.890
But everyone's process is different.

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:34.960
Some writers, I'm sure yours is different.

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:36.410
- Do it is your main piece of advice,

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:38.010
just sit down and do it.
- Right.

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:39.298
- Allocate certain time.

00:16:39.298 --> 00:16:40.718
- Whatever your way is, right.

00:16:40.718 --> 00:16:42.831
I'm not here to tell you,
oh, you have to do it,

00:16:42.831 --> 00:16:45.130
yeah, precisely, I'm not here to tell you

00:16:45.130 --> 00:16:46.470
you have to do it my way.

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:49.140
I'm sure your way, Pete, of
writing is different than mine.

00:16:49.140 --> 00:16:50.840
Maybe you picked it up a
couple times during the day.

00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:52.710
Maybe you'd have a week
where you wrote hard core

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:54.770
and then you take a week
off, whatever that might be.

00:16:54.770 --> 00:16:56.950
You have to do what works in your brain,

00:16:56.950 --> 00:16:59.198
what works in your rhythm with the world.

00:16:59.198 --> 00:17:02.470
But what I really did love
about it that I didn't know

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:04.780
is the discipline of doing it,

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:07.780
it forced me to think
through my issues better,

00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:09.950
to really understand
why I thought the things

00:17:09.950 --> 00:17:12.750
and I gotta tell ya,
man, when the box arrived

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:15.220
and you see this thing, you
see this physical thing,

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:16.638
and you're like--

00:17:16.638 --> 00:17:18.250
- [Pete] I know, it's pretty amazing.

00:17:18.250 --> 00:17:19.083
- I did that?

00:17:19.083 --> 00:17:21.880
And then as you know,
Clyde literally ate the box

00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:24.068
of the first copy and
the first copy of my book

00:17:24.068 --> 00:17:25.787
is eaten by the dog.

00:17:25.787 --> 00:17:27.490
(laughing)
So here we are.

00:17:27.490 --> 00:17:30.340
- [Pete] That's not a
referendum on the book, though.

00:17:30.340 --> 00:17:31.588
- Eh, you know.

00:17:31.588 --> 00:17:35.930
- So, is there anything that you became,

00:17:35.930 --> 00:17:38.350
that you changed your mind
about when you wrote the book?

00:17:38.350 --> 00:17:40.560
Or is there anything that
you, and I'm asking these

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:42.450
questions 'cause we're doing chapter 10

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:44.030
and it's the conclusion of the book.

00:17:44.030 --> 00:17:47.670
Is there anything that you
became more convinced of

00:17:47.670 --> 00:17:49.420
through the process of thinking about it

00:17:49.420 --> 00:17:50.470
and writing the book?

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:54.920
- Gosh, you know, the chapter...

00:17:56.900 --> 00:17:57.890
Let me see.

00:17:57.890 --> 00:18:02.150
You know, truly, the key to
this was really making sure

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:06.360
that what I say and what I've
been preaching on the show

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:09.020
and on Twitter and everywhere
else is really what I believe.

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:11.420
That really was the
key to the whole thing.

00:18:11.420 --> 00:18:15.590
And I did find myself
challenging some of my thoughts.

00:18:15.590 --> 00:18:18.410
So the hardest one for me
to write, in many ways,

00:18:18.410 --> 00:18:22.660
was the abortion chapter,
or the section on abortion,

00:18:22.660 --> 00:18:26.650
because I think you're probably feeling

00:18:26.650 --> 00:18:27.820
a certain version of this too,

00:18:27.820 --> 00:18:30.540
as the Left has gone so off the deep end

00:18:30.540 --> 00:18:32.960
on the pro-choice side,
meaning eight-month abortions,

00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:34.800
let's talk about post-birth abortions.

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:36.790
I mean, just sort of all sorts of stuff.

00:18:36.790 --> 00:18:38.480
And you're pro-choice, right?

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:39.530
- Yes.

00:18:39.530 --> 00:18:42.730
- Yeah, so, but as let's say our team

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:44.890
or our old team, whatever
you wanna call that,

00:18:44.890 --> 00:18:49.160
as they've lost any sense
of what would be moderate,

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:51.890
and by the way, when I interviewed

00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:54.380
several of the candidates,
the more moderate candidates,

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:56.000
Tulsi Gabbard, Andrew Yang,

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:57.230
when I asked them about abortion,

00:18:57.230 --> 00:19:00.390
they really wouldn't say,
well, Yang, particularly,

00:19:00.390 --> 00:19:01.980
wouldn't give me a cut-off point.

00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:04.970
Tulsi sort of said the
first trimester thing,

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:06.670
which I think is fair
and that's the position

00:19:06.670 --> 00:19:08.850
I lay out in the book and by the way,

00:19:08.850 --> 00:19:11.850
I make a point of saying
I do believe it's a life

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:13.590
but we have to have some parameters

00:19:13.590 --> 00:19:15.490
to have all sorts of people
with all sorts of thoughts

00:19:15.490 --> 00:19:20.490
in the country to have some
sort of cohesive society.

00:19:21.260 --> 00:19:24.110
So I really had to challenge
myself through that one

00:19:24.110 --> 00:19:27.290
and as you know, we're in
the process of surrogacy now

00:19:27.290 --> 00:19:30.200
so we're looking at egg
donors and we were getting

00:19:30.200 --> 00:19:33.270
our surrogate and all that
so I was intimately involved,

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:37.380
I'm literally going to
the clinic to donate sperm

00:19:37.380 --> 00:19:39.300
and they're talking to us
about how they're gonna

00:19:39.300 --> 00:19:41.710
take that and put it with
the egg and then put it

00:19:41.710 --> 00:19:42.990
in the surrogate and all these things.

00:19:42.990 --> 00:19:45.950
I was literally going through
that as I was writing it.

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:49.370
And I would liken that
to when people ask me

00:19:49.370 --> 00:19:52.230
about my libertarian economic beliefs,

00:19:52.230 --> 00:19:57.100
and small government and low
taxes and things like that,

00:19:57.100 --> 00:20:00.920
it's like, those ideas, they
were first ideas for me.

00:20:00.920 --> 00:20:03.710
But then, as you know,
I've built a really nice

00:20:03.710 --> 00:20:05.920
small business here and I've been able

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:07.750
to treat my employees well.

00:20:07.750 --> 00:20:10.110
And I love that and my
guys work hard for me

00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:12.820
and we try to take care of
them and I know that if,

00:20:12.820 --> 00:20:16.150
I live in one of the highest
taxed, most regulated states

00:20:16.150 --> 00:20:18.780
in the country if not the
highest on both fronts,

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:21.300
and I know that if the
government would actually

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:25.270
cut my taxes, I could pay my
guys more or hire more people.

00:20:25.270 --> 00:20:27.610
So I think, really, the
rubber met the road, I think,

00:20:27.610 --> 00:20:28.443
as I wrote this.

00:20:28.443 --> 00:20:31.480
I started going, whoa, these
issues that I talk about,

00:20:31.480 --> 00:20:33.079
they're not just things I talk about.

00:20:33.079 --> 00:20:33.912
(dog barking)

00:20:33.912 --> 00:20:34.745
Now Clyde's freaking out.

00:20:34.745 --> 00:20:36.550
They're not just things that I talk about.

00:20:36.550 --> 00:20:38.457
(Pete laughing)

00:20:38.457 --> 00:20:41.230
They're actually ideas that I
put into place in my own life

00:20:41.230 --> 00:20:42.980
and I'm very proud of that.

00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:43.813
- Well, good, good.

00:20:43.813 --> 00:20:47.690
Well, speaking of dogs,
we adopted a dog, too,

00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:50.000
she's right here and barking

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:51.510
so excuse me if you can hear her.

00:20:51.510 --> 00:20:53.540
So, what is the thing,

00:20:53.540 --> 00:20:55.310
we're coming up to the
end of our 20 minutes,

00:20:55.310 --> 00:20:56.670
I think we're a little--
(dog yowling)

00:20:56.670 --> 00:20:58.064
- Yeah.
- There she is.

00:20:58.064 --> 00:20:59.583
- Yeah, she's in on it, too, boy.

00:21:00.740 --> 00:21:02.597
- Here, hold on a second.

00:21:02.597 --> 00:21:05.140
Come here.
(Dave laughing)

00:21:05.140 --> 00:21:07.082
- Mostly, people just
want, yeah, what a beauty,

00:21:07.082 --> 00:21:08.070
what a beauty.

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:09.570
And you got her because of the lockdown,

00:21:09.570 --> 00:21:10.640
I love that, I love that.

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:12.207
- Yeah, we got her for my daughter.

00:21:12.207 --> 00:21:15.390
The day that we got her,
just parenthetically,

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:18.380
the day that we got her,
literally the day, Dave,

00:21:18.380 --> 00:21:20.887
she became sick and had to go to the ICU

00:21:20.887 --> 00:21:23.924
and it cost us $1,000
a day for seven days.

00:21:23.924 --> 00:21:26.230
But she's great, she's been a joy

00:21:26.230 --> 00:21:27.554
and she's part of the family.

00:21:27.554 --> 00:21:29.130
(clearing throat) Excuse
me, she's wonderful.

00:21:29.130 --> 00:21:34.130
Okay, so, chapter 10, I love
the advice about the devices.

00:21:35.120 --> 00:21:37.740
I love the advice about
thinking back about regrets

00:21:37.740 --> 00:21:41.400
and what is the most
meaningful things in our lives.

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:44.080
And I'm gonna try to do that.

00:21:44.080 --> 00:21:46.060
I think I will get an alarm clock.

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:47.590
I was gonna ask you about that.

00:21:47.590 --> 00:21:49.210
And I think I will be,

00:21:49.210 --> 00:21:51.320
'cause I notice that
when I have my devices,

00:21:51.320 --> 00:21:53.900
I'm less present even at the dog park

00:21:53.900 --> 00:21:55.860
or walking the dog or--
- Yeah.

00:21:55.860 --> 00:21:58.650
- I do think it takes something meaningful

00:21:58.650 --> 00:22:03.650
out of those relationships
and it makes you less mindful

00:22:03.750 --> 00:22:06.990
in the moment as well so I
think that there are some things

00:22:06.990 --> 00:22:09.350
that we can all benefit from.

00:22:09.350 --> 00:22:11.950
And I also liked the, I
want to reiterate if people

00:22:11.950 --> 00:22:13.710
are thinking about writing a book,

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:16.968
I think your advice is fantastic,
you just have to do it.

00:22:16.968 --> 00:22:18.990
Perfection is the enemy of done,

00:22:18.990 --> 00:22:21.070
don't get caught up in
this stuff, just sit down.

00:22:21.070 --> 00:22:24.230
And I'll add to what you
said, by telling people

00:22:24.230 --> 00:22:26.660
in your life I'm committed to
this, I'm gonna write a book.

00:22:26.660 --> 00:22:29.300
So, you had the support of your husband

00:22:29.300 --> 00:22:33.550
who slid food to you and so
I think if you tell people

00:22:33.550 --> 00:22:35.427
in your life, like, "I wanna write a book,

00:22:35.427 --> 00:22:37.430
"this means something to me."

00:22:37.430 --> 00:22:39.810
And then I like the
idea that when you said

00:22:39.810 --> 00:22:42.570
that you thought about and reflected on

00:22:42.570 --> 00:22:46.290
abortion or things in your life
that, when you write often,

00:22:46.290 --> 00:22:48.930
it can help clarify or crystalize things.

00:22:48.930 --> 00:22:51.910
What do you wanna leave
people with after they read,

00:22:51.910 --> 00:22:53.770
again, I'm (coughing) excuse
me, asking this question

00:22:53.770 --> 00:22:55.250
'cause it's chapter 10.

00:22:55.250 --> 00:22:58.303
People read this, what do
you wanna leave them with?

00:22:59.580 --> 00:23:01.040
- Truly, what I wanna leave them with,

00:23:01.040 --> 00:23:02.950
and I try to say this at the end,

00:23:02.950 --> 00:23:05.090
and it was exactly why I had the length

00:23:05.090 --> 00:23:06.493
that I had and everything else,

00:23:06.493 --> 00:23:08.970
is that I wanted people to
be able to read this thing

00:23:08.970 --> 00:23:10.450
in a couple days.

00:23:10.450 --> 00:23:12.140
I wanted them to start thinking about

00:23:12.140 --> 00:23:13.450
the issues that I talk about.

00:23:13.450 --> 00:23:15.240
I wanted to give them some tricks

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:16.990
and tips to stand up to the mob.

00:23:16.990 --> 00:23:19.820
I wanted them to be brave enough to fight

00:23:19.820 --> 00:23:21.320
for what they believe in.

00:23:21.320 --> 00:23:22.660
You don't have to agree with me.

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:23.810
I know a lot of conservatives

00:23:23.810 --> 00:23:25.830
are not gonna agree with me on abortion.

00:23:25.830 --> 00:23:26.663
Right?

00:23:26.663 --> 00:23:27.702
We know that.

00:23:27.702 --> 00:23:29.900
And that's okay, by the way.

00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:31.530
And by the way, I'm getting
a ton of email right now

00:23:31.530 --> 00:23:32.617
from conservatives who are saying,

00:23:32.617 --> 00:23:34.857
"Dave, I love the book,
love everything in it,

00:23:34.857 --> 00:23:36.150
"but you're wrong on abortion and

00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:37.250
we'll get you one day."

00:23:37.250 --> 00:23:38.750
But they do it kindly and nicely

00:23:38.750 --> 00:23:39.820
and that's a beautiful thing.

00:23:39.820 --> 00:23:41.950
But what I want more than anything else

00:23:41.950 --> 00:23:44.550
is for someone that reads this book,

00:23:44.550 --> 00:23:46.490
if it hits you in any way,

00:23:46.490 --> 00:23:49.150
I know that means that
there's someone in your life

00:23:49.150 --> 00:23:50.960
that you should hand this thing to.

00:23:50.960 --> 00:23:54.620
And if you hand it to
them with some grace,

00:23:54.620 --> 00:23:57.687
and not club them over the
head with it, but just say,

00:23:57.687 --> 00:23:59.227
"Ah, you know, this was
sort of interesting,

00:23:59.227 --> 00:24:00.597
"the guy kind of agrees with you

00:24:00.597 --> 00:24:01.737
"on this, this, this, and this.

00:24:01.737 --> 00:24:03.650
"But you might not agree with this."

00:24:03.650 --> 00:24:07.100
They might read it and then maybe, maybe,

00:24:07.100 --> 00:24:08.510
we can start fixing this thing.

00:24:08.510 --> 00:24:10.500
And I think we're already doing it

00:24:10.500 --> 00:24:12.420
but our work is cut out for us.

00:24:12.420 --> 00:24:14.440
- Yeah, you know, when I opened the book,

00:24:14.440 --> 00:24:19.016
thank you, your remarks
were totally lovely.

00:24:19.016 --> 00:24:19.849
(coughing) Excuse me.

00:24:19.849 --> 00:24:21.390
When I opened the book and I saw

00:24:21.390 --> 00:24:24.287
that it was devoted to Ben
Affleck, I just lost it.

00:24:24.287 --> 00:24:25.120
(Dave laughing)

00:24:25.120 --> 00:24:26.930
I couldn't stop laughing.

00:24:26.930 --> 00:24:29.308
And I wanna bookmark
that with the last thing

00:24:29.308 --> 00:24:32.990
that you wrote, the last line,
because I think it's so true.

00:24:32.990 --> 00:24:36.470
The book started humorously,
it made me laugh,

00:24:36.470 --> 00:24:38.130
and it just made me think
through the whole way

00:24:38.130 --> 00:24:39.523
and it's just so clear.

00:24:40.885 --> 00:24:43.537
The last thing you write,
the last sentence is,

00:24:43.537 --> 00:24:45.927
"Thinking for yourself is all you need

00:24:45.927 --> 00:24:48.470
"in an age of unreason."

00:24:48.470 --> 00:24:53.470
I mean, that is so true and
so profound at the same time.

00:24:53.870 --> 00:24:57.190
But yet it's so difficult
for all of us, right?

00:24:57.190 --> 00:24:58.340
To think for ourselves.

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:01.300
Right, to be honest with ourselves,

00:25:01.300 --> 00:25:02.940
to be sincere with other people,

00:25:02.940 --> 00:25:05.880
to be forthright in our
speech, to say what we mean.

00:25:05.880 --> 00:25:08.202
And like what you say, to
make sure that what you say

00:25:08.202 --> 00:25:11.330
on the show and the book
and you live what you do,

00:25:11.330 --> 00:25:13.680
it's that idea that you judge a person,

00:25:13.680 --> 00:25:15.150
or we ought to be judged, at least,

00:25:15.150 --> 00:25:17.204
by what we say against what we do.

00:25:17.204 --> 00:25:19.370
And I think that there's something,

00:25:19.370 --> 00:25:21.300
there's a timeless message in that,

00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:26.030
of thinking every age
that we have has some,

00:25:26.030 --> 00:25:27.940
we all are myopic, right?

00:25:27.940 --> 00:25:30.830
We all think that some value,
the values that we have now

00:25:30.830 --> 00:25:33.990
are universal, timeless values.

00:25:33.990 --> 00:25:35.785
But the key is, you said it in the book,

00:25:35.785 --> 00:25:38.130
it's the theme throughout the whole book.

00:25:38.130 --> 00:25:39.963
It's thinking for yourself
and what that means

00:25:39.963 --> 00:25:42.463
and how to be honest with yourself, right?

00:25:43.756 --> 00:25:45.430
- That's it, my friend.

00:25:45.430 --> 00:25:47.125
That was why, as every other author

00:25:47.125 --> 00:25:48.700
in the history of the world,

00:25:48.700 --> 00:25:52.130
I wrote the final sentence about 80 times

00:25:52.130 --> 00:25:55.640
It was like the same idea, 80 times over.

00:25:55.640 --> 00:25:57.570
And I was like, this
is it, no, this is it.

00:25:57.570 --> 00:25:58.410
Could this be funnier?

00:25:58.410 --> 00:25:59.430
No, it's a little too serious.

00:25:59.430 --> 00:26:00.840
Emphasis on this word.

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:03.670
But that was the takeaway,
the takeaway was you don't

00:26:03.670 --> 00:26:07.420
have to buy everything that I'm selling,

00:26:07.420 --> 00:26:08.830
figure it out for yourself.

00:26:08.830 --> 00:26:11.050
And that's it.

00:26:11.050 --> 00:26:12.770
- Figure it out for yourself
and if someone comes

00:26:12.770 --> 00:26:15.515
to a different conclusion,
that's okay, too.

00:26:15.515 --> 00:26:18.353
That's the beginning of the conversation.

00:26:20.330 --> 00:26:22.186
- That's it, my friend.

00:26:22.186 --> 00:26:25.040
- I love it, I can't stress
it enough, truly fantastic

00:26:25.040 --> 00:26:27.950
and it's an honor to be with
you and talk through it.

00:26:27.950 --> 00:26:30.190
Dave, thank you so much and
thank you for your friendship

00:26:30.190 --> 00:26:31.550
and what you do, thank you.

00:26:31.550 --> 00:26:33.530
- Right back at you,
brother, I'll see you soon,

00:26:33.530 --> 00:26:34.850
hopefully in real life.

00:26:34.850 --> 00:26:36.295
Eventually in real life, right?

00:26:36.295 --> 00:26:38.320
- Eventually.
- Maybe one day, perhaps.

00:26:38.320 --> 00:26:40.350
- I'm looking forward to it, thanks, Dave.

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:41.600
You're a good man.
- All right, be good.

